[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083109 :question/id #uuid "ed80b546-956c-5b67-ac7d-0c0049c2278c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7385" :question/problem "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of pets | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $5$ | $6$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $5+6+5=16,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{16}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{16})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{5}{16}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{16}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083113 :question/id #uuid "292925c0-95de-59f4-8888-7073448230d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7759" :question/problem "What is the binomial expansion of $\\dfrac{6}{3-x}$ up to the third term for $\\mid x \\mid < 3$?" :question/worked-solution "The generalized binomial formula states that\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{n(n-1)x^2}{2!} + \\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)x^3}{3!}+\\cdots\n$$\n\nFactoring $3$ out of the binomial, we rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6(3 - x)^{-1} &= 6(3(1 - \\frac{1}{3}x))^{-1} \\\\\n&= 6(3)^{-1}(1 - \\frac{1}{3}x)^{-1} \\\\\n&= 2(1 - \\frac{1}{3}x)^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the generalized binomial formula with $n=-1$ and\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\ninstead of $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{3 - x} &= 2(1 - \\frac{1}{3}x)^{-1} \\\\\n&= 2[1 + (-1)(-\\frac{1}{3}x) + ((-1)(-1 - 1)(-\\frac{1}{3}x)^{2})/(2!) + ⋯] \\\\\n&= 2[1 + \\frac{1}{3}x + ((-1)(-2)(\\frac{1}{9})x^{2})/(2) + ⋯] \\\\\n&= 2[1 + \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{9}x^{2} + ⋯] \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{2}{9}x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083118 :question/id #uuid "1193941c-3af9-56fa-abf8-32a07228f46e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7862" :question/problem "A certain disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|D)P(D) + P(A|D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A|D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{11}+\\frac{12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12}{55} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083127 :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the episodes of a popular podcast is $43\\,\\textrm{min}.$ Additionally, $20\\%$ of the episodes have a duration of less than $38$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of a randomly selected episode is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the episode durations, rounded to the nearest minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ | $\\, 0.15 \\,$ | $\\, 0.20 \\,$ | $\\, 0.25 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, -2.3263 \\,$ | $\\, -1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, -1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, -1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, -1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, -0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, -0.6745 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected episode. Then, we have $X \\sim N(43, \\sigma^2),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma.$ \n\nWe know that $P(X \\leq 38) = 0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 38) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{38-43}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.20) =-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1683 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1683} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈6\\,\\text{minutes.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083128 :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281" :question/problem "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Additionally, $50\\%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.93\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.07\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Find, as a percentage, the proportion of people with a systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.75 \\,$ | $\\, 0.80 \\,$ | $\\, 0.85 \\,$ | $\\, 0.90 \\,$ | $\\, 0.95 \\,$ | $\\, 0.97 \\,$ | $\\, 0.99 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, 0.6745 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, 1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, 1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, 1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, 1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, 2.3263 \\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = \\Phi(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X \\sim N(\\mu, 12^2),$ and we want to find $P(X <  120).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu.$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.93\\mu  <   X <   1.07\\mu ) = 0.5.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.93\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.25 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5+0.25 \\\\ P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ \\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & =0.6745 \\\\ 0.07\\mu  & =8.094 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.094}{0.07} \\\\  & ≈115.629.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $P(X <   120)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<120) & =P(Z<\\frac{120-115.629}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.36) \\\\  & =Φ(0.36) \\\\  & =0.6406 \\\\  & ≈64.1%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $64.1\\%$ of the people have systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083131 :question/id #uuid "a6af5246-ef2e-5b52-8ec7-2bc861b1398d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8757" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083143 :question/id #uuid "c0a47f18-97ca-5bbe-8825-f088318a4cb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9875" :question/problem "Use the root test to show that the following series is convergent.\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac {(7n)^n n} {(8n + 1) ^ n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n =1} ^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na_n \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $L$ is defined as\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\sqrt [n] {a_n} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\left({a_n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L\\lt 1$, the series converges.\n- If $L \\gt 1$ or $L = \\infty$, the series diverges.\n- If $L = 1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence $a_n$, defined as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac {(7n)^n n} {(8n + 1) ^ n}\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{a_n}$ as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞)\\sqrt[n]{a_{n}} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(a_{n})^{1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)[((7n)^{n}n)/((8n + 1)^{n})]^{1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)\\frac{7n \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n + 1} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)((\\frac{7n \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n}}{n}))/((\\frac{8n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(7\\sqrt[n]{n})/((8 + \\frac{1}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7 \\cdot 1}{8 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\sqrt[n]{n}=1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL =\\dfrac{7}{8} \\lt 1\n$$\n\nthe series converges by the root test."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083144 :question/id #uuid "031d607b-0478-5676-ac4b-596431ca9481" :question/math-academy-id "e-9876" :question/problem "Use the root test to show that the following series is divergent.\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{n^45^n}{3^n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n =1} ^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na_n \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $L$ is defined as\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\sqrt [n] {a_n} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\left({a_n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L \\lt 1$, the series converges.\n- If $L \\gt 1$ or $L = \\infty$, the series diverges.\n- If $L = 1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence $a_n$, defined as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n^45^n}{3^n}\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{a_n}$ as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞)\\sqrt[n]{a_{n}} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(a_{n})^{1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{n^{4}5^{n}}{3^{n}})^{1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n^{4}} \\cdot 5}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}lim_(n → ∞)\\sqrt[n]{n^{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot 1^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\sqrt[n]{n} = 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL =\\dfrac{5}{3} \\gt 1\n$$\n\nthe series diverges by the root test."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae82bbb8-6db7-5eaa-8ae8-8e78f0012dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.876" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079794} {:answer/id #uuid "e2660f4a-d419-57f5-ac24-285005d96d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.ty