pe/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375211} {:answer/id #uuid "c93c042d-9dd7-5564-bf71-53483739b88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{248}{625}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375212} {:answer/id #uuid "4d60bb26-2073-57f2-9fd2-bc08881cccdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{496}{625}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6afef0cd-73b0-5c32-8010-c9c097c9f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375209} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be16b83-4c0b-5731-aa48-8aee5e10b222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21f7d13d-467e-59c9-8e92-2f0c7e4df999" :question/math-academy-id "q-67140" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=3.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{124}{125})}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92f7a6d9-4573-56ec-a272-22d51d9677f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082350} {:answer/id #uuid "97b66d3b-d03f-5cc2-8e53-69fa87651b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082351} {:answer/id #uuid "985fa0a2-27e4-55f0-a3e7-ec7eb1eeec04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082352} {:answer/id #uuid "c661ccd4-7b87-5993-9971-92bb783767a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082353} {:answer/id #uuid "dab1416a-bd9f-59cb-ae26-6f70d1e4bb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082354}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2ccfd3-99c0-5b8b-8131-2d4d9ec6ccc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7bdf0d8-351d-5150-9e3f-d6b74ad83da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385475} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9f5be3-653b-5cb2-bc22-588b4016cda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385476} {:answer/id #uuid "a290b05d-93bc-5976-911e-c5b9b644bdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385477} {:answer/id #uuid "233ac7ae-4cbe-5d40-bb71-ec2fce5a0b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385478} {:answer/id #uuid "b860169e-87c7-5b20-80bb-ac38367bfb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a9f5be3-653b-5cb2-bc22-588b4016cda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385476} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd6abb2-1bec-5d23-8439-f0eb6307e987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e56ea2d3-1d8c-5452-b364-28ca562ceb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-67143" :question/problem "Determine the value of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6},r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}] \\\\  & ≈0.200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8534a8d1-25d2-5e7e-8c99-90224b718c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082362} {:answer/id #uuid "a615016f-4445-5a4b-a366-bbb6f14fb84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082363} {:answer/id #uuid "0401c9f0-34fd-574b-af62-c2e16869c9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082364} {:answer/id #uuid "73348e96-cc83-52b6-9669-85f190eeab0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082365} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6956dd-fea9-5a89-8706-94befecb08f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.773" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082366}] :answer-field/id #uuid "256e1ac9-5f1a-5416-ba2c-487b6aa119bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0ac17d3-ec04-5bcb-85e4-e3f87449143f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375229} {:answer/id #uuid "57652154-588e-5704-a612-ce35b36d7983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375230} {:answer/id #uuid "ecba151b-7837-5b72-8b80-211a22822436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375231} {:answer/id #uuid "704abe2c-991c-5113-a9af-ed61b48301f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375232} {:answer/id #uuid "f5454bae-49f5-5133-9434-f686209d7742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.773" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5454bae-49f5-5133-9434-f686209d7742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.773" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375233} :answer-field/id #uuid "a053e6f7-fa3e-5c20-aefb-b5f4797b2795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e0ebd5e-9b64-5c07-820a-486e75fdcfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-67151" :question/problem "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{7}8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{3}{5}.$ Also, note that $N=8,$ since there are $8$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{0-1} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{-1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{40}{3}\\,,r=\\frac{3}{5}$ and $N=8$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}]}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}]}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{3}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{8}] \\\\  & ≈32.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edc6c91-b546-52c1-abe6-c41407063d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082374} {:answer/id #uuid "919505ef-ab05-5d98-b28e-c95695566647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082375} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ba447-050d-5a58-b993-e03e063e2596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082376} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cd4737-e139-5014-9ad2-503cc6e990b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082377} {:answer/id #uuid "14e52f90-6097-5fb9-a840-f6e4099a1cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8edc6c91-b546-52c1-abe6-c41407063d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082374} :answer-field/id #uuid "6375df85-f8a7-5a1a-9c25-0ace54eb1a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed398a32-5acf-5617-b286-7da491ceb0a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-67203" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $18$ and a third term equal to $2.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=18$ and ${a}_{3}=2.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{2}{18} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16dfb55b-8af4-596f-a315-353765522560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333194} {:answer/id #uuid "c16a839e-1b3e-54f0-9222-740ef19de000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334665} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d5d8b0-70e4-5f14-b80f-590b782f7d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334729} {:answer/id #uuid "ce913bb5-7127-5bc9-acac-45bf128689d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334790} {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e0065-d183-5d46-90dd-110f8b159fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73a45f9-70e6-5c98-9255-49149400966d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6808" :question/problem "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28186592-08bc-549e-9c22-c79ee3ea6745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336408} {:answer/id #uuid "3025f42b-c5db-534a-b7db-5eb1874e4c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336493} {:answer/id #uuid "61f846a2-1971-5874-84e8-c682412f989c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336914} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbd7131-4a34-5bb1-b11e-243d31511be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337150} {:answer/id #uuid "a724f86f-6e25-582e-8cc1-02e5086a17ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f846a2-1971-5874-84e8-c682412f989c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336914} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0ed294-f84f-5efe-be0c-4f49c2d7af7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6995c088-70db-5fcb-a926-3a5bb8ce6736" :question/math-academy-id "q-7151" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+7⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+7)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=4,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nv=⟨4,3⟩\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=4$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(4)| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=4$ is $5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169125f0-3e4b-5c6a-b5a1-89b777b83f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348635} {:answer/id #uuid "37ec9671-ab78-519f-b1e7-71d5da25779b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348951} {:answer/id #uuid "55449f33-7665-51fe-9702-f5d7e4e864fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349228} {:answer/id #uuid "73dd5657-1c1c-53ad-ad9d-ac117ac7883a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349511} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2f7a50-e7e8-5a25-86d8-2f5cc19d264e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169125f0-3e4b-5c6a-b5a1-89b777b83f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348635} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89f0117-9472-553a-ba2d-6662ba158e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "697d7677-790c-5ef6-9fc8-07067ff8df87" :question/math-academy-id "q-83642" :question/problem "Several children were asked their age. The age of each child (in years) was recorded below. If $X$ is the age of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Age | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the children's ages, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $2+4+4=10,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10)=f(10) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(X=11)=f(11) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(X=12)=f(12) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =10\\cdot f(10)+11\\cdot f(11)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{5})+11(\\frac{2}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+22+24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{5} \\\\  & =11.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c32360-a98d-5f30-872f-0e66d2befce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351714} {:answer/id #uuid "1954c4b2-3540-5434-8e40-752070a6a38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351791} {:answer/id #uuid "30ddd6ec-ae34-5fc7-85a4-ad2ccad156a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352016} {:answer/id #uuid "6cec3512-5e08-5601-baad-00d6fd86632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352526} {:answer/id #uuid "cba5a6d7-02a7-58bb-a12a-14af46bfd046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cec3512-5e08-5601-baad-00d6fd86632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352526} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d39a15c-1edb-5184-99a1-c6e39d397222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ce848d4-59c3-57f3-83e1-aacd036756e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-88578" :question/problem "In a class, $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of them play soccer, and of the girls, $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them play soccer. What is the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen student plays soccer, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the boys play soccer and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the girls play soccer, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|B)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(S|{B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student plays soccer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|B)P(B)+P(S|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044e54c5-cb3c-5dc6-bf9d-d182940ab0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351607} {:answer/id #uuid "6027b46e-701c-5eee-a24e-ddf6d90d3b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352422} {:answer/id #uuid "6abd7ca8-7930-5b16-9e31-720e95358e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352514} {:answer/id #uuid "c2db2a63-61b8-5dff-bcbe-6ed79ca8bf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353253} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b5f850-914a-53f8-b368-350fbe3a3a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abd7ca8-7930-5b16-9e31-720e95358e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352514} :answer-field/id #uuid "f733c66c-b0df-52a4-a900-debf97b60f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfc184b2-405b-5616-ae5a-9b803be06566" :question/math-academy-id "q-88581" :question/problem "In a class, $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys, and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls. Of the boys, $\\frac{1}{12}$ of them play basketball. The probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15}.$ If a girl is selected at random, what is the probability that she plays basketball?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen student plays basketball, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{12}$ of the boys play basketball, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(A|{B}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{12})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(A|{B}^{′})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{15}) & =60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{′}))​ \\\\ 4 & =3+24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ 1 & =24P(A|{B}^{′}) \\\\ P(A|{B}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected girl plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{24}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c0a8b9-2311-5c4d-bef5-3d7685ef169c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082770} {:answer/id #uuid "b608932c-d173-5916-82ff-f469c95139c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082771} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a73c2-583c-5465-ae68-4578fb2d9626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082772} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca05ae-9324-5547-b52b-1e3f59299631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082773} {:answer/id #uuid "7a757a37-5fa5-5f86-a6be-1f381ed50c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca05ae-9324-5547-b52b-1e3f59299631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082773} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a0d3931-3463-53b0-9a27-b086c39a2042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "540f5969-8bf8-564c-8a5d-794886869947" :question/math-academy-id "q-94697" :question/problem "In a factory, soda cans are filled with a mean volume of $12$ ounces of soda. However, $1%$ of cans contain no more than $11.5$ ounces of soda. Assuming that the volume of soda in each can is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the volume of soda in a can, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 11.5)=0.01.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11.5) & =0.01 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.5-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ -\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.99)={Φ}^{-1}(0.01)=-2.3263.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =-2.3263 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =4.6524 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{4.6524} \\\\  & ≈0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f04dd09-9af6-5af5-b2a7-02412d51615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} {:answer/id #uuid "95a31260-f0cc-565e-b971-d2bd4d135ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2a8b1e-34b7-5bdb-960e-a7263f101f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.2 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082778} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0255ec-0e04-54e7-ae22-0d510b62b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082779} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac1477f-55e1-52bb-abe7-4f577c033faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f04dd09-9af6-5af5-b2a7-02412d51615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082776} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf9b764-d015-5366-9df6-e11472a54a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9debd84a-b5d9-54c6-84fa-986684bdfc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94702" :question/problem "In a factory, cookies contain $15$ grams of sugar on average. However, $10%$ of cookies contain more than $17$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the percent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of grams of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>17)=0.1.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =1-P(X>17) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =0.9 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{17-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)≈1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.6408 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.6408} \\\\  & ≈1.5605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12) & =P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12-15}{1.5605}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.92) \\\\  & =0.0274 \\\\  & ≈2.7%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $2.7%$ of cookies contain less than $12$ grams of sugar."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8462dc0-fe59-552c-bcf7-80ed2890c358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.6 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082788} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c7787a-918e-5955-b4df-9ef94902935b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.6 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082789} {:answer/id #uuid "041698b3-5996-584d-b38a-862c66db9fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.4 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082790} {:answer/id #uuid "481f3b16-46a8-52fb-aa2e-66b42f4dc450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.5 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082791} {:answer/id #uuid "54869635-b277-52f4-a0e7-e0880ef92ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.8 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54869635-b277-52f4-a0e7-e0880ef92ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.8 \\mathrm{\\% }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082792} :answer-field/id #uuid "dba73051-efbb-5280-b980-315535defe6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f827144-d4f5-511d-8441-f8b61a3b9073" :question/math-academy-id "q-95139" :question/problem "A cellphone battery's average time from full to empty is $26$ hours. Additionally, $20%$ of the time, the battery lasts less than $20$ hours. Assuming that the duration of the battery is normally distributed, find the percentage of the times that a full charge lasts more than $30$ hours, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the time a full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(26,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>30).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<20)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<20) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-26}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-6}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.14027 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.14027} \\\\  & ≈7.1291.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(30<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-26}{7.1291}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.56) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.56) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.56) \\\\  & =1-0.7123 \\\\  & ≈28.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $28.8%$ of the times the battery lasts more than $30$ hours."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdc05b80-547e-5221-9bd9-f311a2abf68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$65$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082794} {:answer/id #uuid "cb38bc56-8538-553f-bc91-f55e890f8181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$63$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082795} {:answer/id #uuid "45a364c8-1fde-5e7a-9b91-72883c7924a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$60$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082796} {:answer/id #uuid "605283b3-ade6-5082-b448-e578520aa643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$70$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082797} {:answer/id #uuid "75102103-d220-59fb-9c25-d0cffe00997c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$55$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb38bc56-8538-553f-bc91-f55e890f8181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$63$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082795} :answer-field/id #uuid "5040927c-1437-52da-b383-cc4ee8d378da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fe4bcec-e92b-527f-8615-838309a0dd15" :question/math-academy-id "q-95154" :question/problem "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<59)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<59) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 59-\\mu  & =-4.208 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈63\\,\\text{days.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087438 :question/id #uuid "7a4196bc-1f72-5435-9c00-c1fbef60b549" :question/math-academy-id "e-14710" :question/problem "Write $y = 7x+\\dfrac{2}{5}$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087474 :question/id #uuid "49e49d7f-2dda-5492-8aa7-e8a92e4a6067" :question/math-academy-id "e-3659" :question/problem "Given that $\\sin{\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}< \\theta < \\pi$, what is the value of $\\sin2\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{2}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087475 :question/id #uuid "41c17be0-26f5-512a-879f-7e94fcc7e008" :question/math-academy-id "e-3662" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\csc{2\\theta}$ if $\\cos{\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{2\\theta}}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $\\sin{2\\theta}$ and then compute its reciprocal.\n\nTo find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta \\lt 0$, and we take the *negative* root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt 3}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 2θ &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 2θ} = (1)/((-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})) =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087476 :question/id #uuid "d8889f85-b0d9-5c84-b0ae-b04c3c0aafff" :question/math-academy-id "e-3665" :question/problem "Given that $\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $0^\\circ < \\theta < 90^{\\circ}$, what is the value of $\\sin{2\\theta}$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the value of $\\sin{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\sin\\theta > 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477 :question/id #uuid "7826cd52-8603-51ff-902e-c7764639dccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3666" :question/problem "Express $\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)} - \\sin^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087478 :question/id #uuid "2b9dc462-ec26-5762-aa64-d9e8f16112c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3667" :question/problem "Express $2\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087483 :question/id #uuid "4eb0f179-c5e1-5935-8ff0-9e65005cbb16" :question/math-academy-id "e-3678" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{\\tan^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) - 1}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the given expression by $(-1)$ and rewrite it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(\\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5}) - 1) &= (-2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(1 - \\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5})) \\\\\n&= - (2\\tan (\\frac{π}{5}))/(1 - \\tan^{2} (\\frac{π}{5}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1 - \\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)}{1-\\tan^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right)} = -\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087487 :question/id #uuid "9bcc5594-02dc-594f-9f7e-5e0700118ecf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3682" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{5\\tan{2x}}{\\cot{x}-\\tan{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\ninto our expression and then simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5\\tan 2x}{\\cot x - \\tan x} &= (5\\tan 2x)/((\\frac{1}{\\tan x} - \\tan x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5\\tan 2x \\cdot \\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x} \\\\\n&= 5\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan {2x} = \\dfrac {2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}) &= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\frac{2\\tan x}{1 - \\tan^{2} x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan 2x \\cdot (\\tan 2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\tan^{2} 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\tan{2x}}{\\cot{x}-\\tan{x}} = \\dfrac{5}{2} \\tan^2 {2x}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :question/id #uuid "69cb7589-c985-5937-bc2c-1fb1d8cadf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3699" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{8\\tan{2\\theta}}{1-\\tan^2{2\\theta}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{2\\tan x}{1-\\tan^2 x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan 4θ = \\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}\n$$\n\nWriting our expression in terms of $\\tan 2\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ} &= 4 \\cdot (\\frac{2\\tan 2θ}{1 - \\tan^{2} 2θ}) = 4\\tan 4θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\tan{2\\theta}}{1-\\tan^2{2\\theta}} = 4\\tan{4\\theta}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087519 :question/id #uuid "b4055943-40a7-5920-9850-a9f1f50da9c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5089" :question/problem "Express $z=-5+12\\textrm{i}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta).$ Round $\\theta$ to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z = -5+12\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(-5)}^{2}+{(12)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = -5$ is negative and $y = 12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the second quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{12}{-5}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\  & ≈1.966,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z = -5 + 12\\textrm{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = 13(\\cos(1.966)+\\textrm{i}\\sin(1.966))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087520 :question/id #uuid "e50fe1bc-2a1d-5913-b9d7-013c05baf246" :question/math-academy-id "e-5090" :question/problem "Express $z=4\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)$ in the form $z=x+\\textrm{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will simplify the right-hand side. Since $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087521 :question/id #uuid "3b103330-6aa7-5f1a-9a83-808515a43f97" :question/math-academy-id "e-5091" :question/problem "Express $z=\\dfrac{3}{2-\\textrm{i}}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\textrm{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\textrm{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $2+\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(2+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = \\dfrac{6}{5}$ and $y = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0.464,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087541 :question/id #uuid "339f9406-2f1a-52de-959b-23a7fe73ef68" :question/math-academy-id "e-5282" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=4+4\\textrm{i}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta = \\arg(z),$ we first calculate $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into $z=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ we get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087557 :question/id #uuid "961ff66e-ce82-5ef7-bd5e-d5aa14c1ec58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5379" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087559 :question/id #uuid "aa0bc73b-1454-5dd2-a77c-4020c56e5c71" :question/math-academy-id "e-5406" :question/problem "A curve is defined parametrically by the equations $x=\\dfrac{1}{t}, y=\\sqrt{(t-2)(3t-1)}$. What are the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts of the curve?" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$(t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2$.\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$(3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/((\\frac{1}{3})); = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087572 :question/id #uuid "da4e2c42-894b-5f2a-917b-a5df22f41019" :question/math-academy-id "e-5754" :question/problem "Determine if $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)=-6x^2+11x-4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the factor theorem, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x = \\dfrac{b}{a}$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(4-3x).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x & =0 \\\\ 4 & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, $(4-3x)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nSo, we just need to check if $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of $p(x).$ In other words, we need to check if $p\\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) =0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{4}{3}) & =-6{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+11(\\frac{4}{3})-4 \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{44}{3}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+44-12}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, $p \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)=0,$ so $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is a root of the polynomial. Consequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(4-3x)$ is indeed a factor of $p(x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087573 :question/id #uuid "a930b12e-d598-584e-82cc-63efb6c91e25" :question/math-academy-id "e-5773" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=2+7\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<      \\arg(z)<      \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})≈1.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087589 :question/id #uuid "d1a9b27b-80e7-53af-add8-4c740d750746" :question/math-academy-id "e-6224" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087628 :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/0a/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ 8 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087635 :question/id #uuid "e29f6eb6-9b2a-5f03-aad3-3bc36dbe400e" :question/math-academy-id "e-756" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the expansion of $f(x) = \\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{1+2x}{1+x}\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\ln(1+x),$ we use the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\ln{(1+2x)},$ we replace $x$ with $2x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x) & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the two series to give the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) \\\\  & =(2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯)-(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the series $\\ln(1+2x),$ the radius of convergence is given by\n\n$$\n-1 < 2x \\leq 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right].\n$$\n\nThe series $\\ln(1+x)$ has a radius of convergence of $x\\in(-1,1].$ Therefore, the radius of convergence of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] \\cap (-1,1] = \\left(-\\dfrac12,\\dfrac12\\right] .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087637 :question/id #uuid "16a3ed8f-fc1f-5443-909a-07b0f99a7c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-758" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $1+\\dfrac{2}{3} + \\dfrac{4}{9} + \\dfrac{8}{27} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{2^n}{3^n}+\\cdots\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\dfrac{2}{3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f({\\color{blue}x}) = \\dfrac{1}{1-{\\color{blue}x}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ 3 & =1+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087638 :question/id #uuid "5a3f6da3-e6c7-5f71-9167-01e0bdc654e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-761" :question/problem "Find the sum $\\, \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{(2/3)^2}{2} + \\dfrac{(2/3)^3}{3} + \\cdots +\\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^n}{n}+\\cdots\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = \\ln(1+x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\frac{2}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087643 :question/id #uuid "d633035c-c1c0-55ea-9e6f-53d7dd18a01f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8073" :question/problem "Given that $\\csc \\theta = 2$ and $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant, what is the value of $\\sin 2\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $\\sin2\\theta$, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of $\\cos{\\theta}$. From the Pythagorean identity, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, $\\cos\\theta \\gt 0$, and we take the *positive* square root in the above expression. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values for sine and cosine into the double-angle formula for sine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 2θ &= 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4514e843-dc0c-5be5-9e96-42ad1bfc870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{{v}^{2}+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272978} {:answer/id #uuid "680783c7-a79b-52f6-8c63-e5a4705ea03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0d77cc-d328-5023-810b-e3a1f565a8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273437} {:answer/id #uuid "b9914a60-1aeb-5bf6-9bfb-f20416011265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{3}-{v}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273999} {:answer/id #uuid "f794ae15-8082-5a6e-b5d7-76a88fb45ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{{v}^{2}+v}{3{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680783c7-a79b-52f6-8c63-e5a4705ea03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273285} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c0796e-4620-5dae-9cfd-a686aee98f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695b49d1-9327-503a-b244-1f7f385f59ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14315" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e8f198-1363-57d1-9751-734d4becc83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281861} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2d7447-386b-5d65-9b31-e03151e355ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281928} {:answer/id #uuid "31340472-87e0-5a8c-9cba-26cb27beb8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\,\\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282131} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7bde5e-0acf-5074-8b46-bbc63bfd01e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.464\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282763} {:answer/id #uuid "b7526945-d7f5-5e1d-be7a-4e69e3f6a671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.644\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e8f198-1363-57d1-9751-734d4becc83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281861} :answer-field/id #uuid "54839742-5697-584f-be10-bed1b81a6469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41ba3c7b-634a-5f48-9b9b-3e5652d0eb21" :question/math-academy-id "q-17517" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7eb4042a5422d631c867cba1c1fa9c52b400d0061fd5fe7d2fa380d721dbac5.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =4+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.644.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=5$ and $\\theta =0.644$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=5\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.644\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4ba8cf-44b3-5246-b1ff-3908e09be4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282335} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe4a494-9d38-5337-884e-6379ac4e87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{0.034\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282501} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bc98f-3937-5670-9838-94b365aac670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282637} {:answer/id #uuid "b84c9579-649d-5618-9320-d193030846d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283282} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3bc366-a7d8-5bb8-b26a-6174de216c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4ba8cf-44b3-5246-b1ff-3908e09be4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282335} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a2edc2-0190-53f6-a3c8-1c5ab94b34a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a6545b0-3575-5163-bc0b-11c66105aac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17518" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=-1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{-1})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈2.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =2.034$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.034\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04c8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085089} {:answer/id #uuid "da5a2b38-cb05-5079-8a67-44b3023a6a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085090} {:answer/id #uuid "011ca4e6-3122-58c6-a064-72bf61a7cc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx^{2}}(yx^{2}) = x^{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085091} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc23e01-156d-58f7-838b-e84160a81d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y}{x}) = \\frac{\\cos x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085092} {:answer/id #uuid "6436279c-38d0-5c1d-a595-b7ab9c80521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085093}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c0749d-dee0-5f31-b150-55643e10de7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085088}] :question/id #uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17540" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos x$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c3fa80-be85-5fb6-af9c-0b3a67fee784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2}) = \\frac{5}{2}x - \\frac{65}{84}x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085119} {:answer/id #uuid "9cffe1e8-afca-5e05-a3ac-8704d2799ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2}) = \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{65}{21}x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085120} {:answer/id #uuid "cdebd8f0-1687-5401-965d-991aefca5a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2}) = \\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{7}{8}x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085121} {:answer/id #uuid "e9459570-482f-59c0-8770-c6162ed75f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2}) = \\frac{5}{2}x - \\frac{65}{48}x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085122} {:answer/id #uuid "732ed59e-60ab-53bd-944d-04eef95dca11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2}) = \\frac{2}{5}x - 3x^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085123}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b68d46be-92a4-5692-9c8e-f3ce5994d8b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085118}] :question/id #uuid "121a8214-e611-5039-9946-1ccc7d7da770" :question/math-academy-id "q-18951" :question/problem "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\sin (2x) + \\sin (\\frac{x}{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e5beee-cd5d-5d0e-a06c-ed2bcb868f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+10y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e5beee-cd5d-5d0e-a06c-ed2bcb868f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+10y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525416} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca145851-e9d5-53d0-b524-37f7de7eeb0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c83729c-0f7e-5ffe-bf01-d09e27351dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c83729c-0f7e-5ffe-bf01-d09e27351dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525515} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f29fa38-38be-5d7a-8b04-017c959f4e64" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b90758e4-7aff-5ee7-bbb0-64e72c86781c" :question/math-academy-id "q-208783" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{2}$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}x+y=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}x+y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}x+y) & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 2x+10y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+10y=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075d6c0f-91ae-5d3a-8904-f94acdc0ce84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288092} {:answer/id #uuid "250beb4e-89fe-54a1-a69b-97059dc5f771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288272} {:answer/id #uuid "4169b527-77fc-5467-b408-c2fac538f2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288476} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd0eaa1-713d-59b2-aa0a-54b382724e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288584} {:answer/id #uuid "af22ea99-4405-5cb4-84cc-edd0522c77e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4169b527-77fc-5467-b408-c2fac538f2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288476} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c1b968-b21c-5ef9-be91-d01d0d0f5cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eda88667-03a3-54d2-bc45-5bd8cd910c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-24488" :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7088b1af-54df-5c95-bec1-e96b53c5f1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288815} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca70449-d3c0-500a-bf01-9fc59bdb8e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289086} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1691b5-d526-5306-8cf2-8c5f601c7ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289165} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db6555-c3dc-5b31-9920-d1231647197e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289533} {:answer/id #uuid "e474e08f-fd81-5207-ab8d-f763fcb42bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e474e08f-fd81-5207-ab8d-f763fcb42bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da4ca44-fffb-53ef-ac9c-e81ad6738ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a18ec028-af86-5940-bde5-98a13535f55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-24490" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0043b9dd-acd7-5722-ae3f-3f717ea8f717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288026} {:answer/id #uuid "0fcd1b2f-6684-5235-9ad9-1439089c5d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288147} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93b461-8b28-554d-a5d3-b0197fe38c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288346} {:answer/id #uuid "38664848-58db-58e9-955b-e673ca767957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288415} {:answer/id #uuid "679b2481-2702-5446-9e64-41947ec19124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679b2481-2702-5446-9e64-41947ec19124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288759} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e2b6c24-7b3b-5d04-808b-ab04891dc6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baeab088-833a-54f7-96df-e4a2e1a80c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-24514" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b2bb68-89cb-59c8-9356-f76f21ec7260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b2bb68-89cb-59c8-9356-f76f21ec7260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293529} :answer-field/id #uuid "0415426c-2704-586e-93d9-ec13672b3656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d11d0095-5740-5d82-8a0d-f23a175699b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279153" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b12eb6672d263655f9f77eaafbf486a7be5bf9b7673c73fd0d75382d192a658b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{9}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{81}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125c2c5c-61b7-5d55-8f98-6b218c5c51da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557907} {:answer/id #uuid "27dc9e0c-011e-521d-8ad7-d9d93470042c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=15+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557945} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2eab43-a851-5fcc-9855-5ebae7ded852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558079} {:answer/id #uuid "98bd8735-0b1c-52c7-88b3-8d0c169afb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=15-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558164} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e836cf-bbc1-5aba-b0f6-de39516f1a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125c2c5c-61b7-5d55-8f98-6b218c5c51da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=5-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557907} :answer-field/id #uuid "51909d3e-6b80-5264-9fd1-f708fe7f41a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46eecd6b-234b-5d91-be76-8bf8679a9744" :question/math-academy-id "q-47332" :question/problem "Given the curve $y=5x-4{x}^{2}$, calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ using the definition of the derivative." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}.$ The definition of ${y}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(5(x+h)-4(x+h)^{2})-(5x-4{x}^{2})}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5x+5h-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}-5x+4{x}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(5-8x-4h)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(5-8x-4h) \\\\  & =5-8x-4(0) \\\\  & =5-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06159cb3-2361-56dd-ac93-ef60bc1de311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{3x^{2}}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085801} {:answer/id #uuid "132ff5b3-259f-535f-a8d3-05cd841983df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)(((x + h)^{2} - 3x^{2})/(h^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085802} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccb533c-abe0-51f8-b1cd-cf2a89585b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)((3x^{2} - (x + h)^{2})/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085803} {:answer/id #uuid "3734e405-1ef0-59be-9b95-d045f0e4a227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)((3(x + h)^{2} - 3x^{2})/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085804} {:answer/id #uuid "57fe166b-8424-54d4-a1d7-af21c44d55a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)(((x + h) + x)/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085805}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1d14c006-27e7-5a1c-9e6f-d3e243f074be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085800}] :question/id #uuid "238c536f-ac91-5a9f-b669-762b3eb9af59" :question/math-academy-id "q-47876" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 3x^{2}$, what is $f^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6debf7-670d-51a9-ab65-69167ec49d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)((9(x + h) + 9x^{3})/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085807} {:answer/id #uuid "bd833841-1c5c-506f-8544-9a84ecb5884c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{9x^{3}}{h^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085808} {:answer/id #uuid "2943dfb3-5a6e-5d83-9ced-858492ca43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)((3x^{9} - (x + h)^{9})/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085809} {:answer/id #uuid "29a8adf2-1501-507f-8b7e-b9f518dea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)((9(x + h)^{3} - 9x^{3})/(h))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085810} {:answer/id #uuid "3935e0b9-8285-511d-b3d1-8daff8906fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(h → 0)(((x + h)^{2} - 3x^{2})/(h^{9}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085811}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af6b2a90-5a3e-5ced-8010-3df5a622b4dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085806}] :question/id #uuid "ff52c38d-e26e-5262-b203-db92520a4b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47877" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = 9x^{3}$, what is $f^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf5e817-a3bd-539e-8584-daafc704637d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085873} {:answer/id #uuid "40c8f5ea-7d31-5595-a27e-ade25e88bbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085874} {:answer/id #uuid "43ac4ba3-0060-5140-ac1a-dd226fa32ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\ln 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085875} {:answer/id #uuid "4f239308-52c1-5f9a-9cb1-0c23050686d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref